WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:06.610
Usually when we talk about how the 2020 Democratic
primary is looking, we talk about national

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:13.340
polls and in national polls, Joe Biden has
and is leading followed by Elizabeth Warren

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.920
and Bernie Sanders who have sort of been trading
off second and third place.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.230
But that's national polling and in practice,
the primaries don't happen all at once.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.570
They don't happen all on one day.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.000
The first caucus is February 3rd in Iowa.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.960
The first primary is February 11th in New
Hampshire.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.559
And so New Hampshire is a very important early
state in setting the tone of the primaries.

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:39.030
But at the same time, we don't want to give
New Hampshire more importance than it actually

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.030
has.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.579
Because remember that in 2016 Bernie Sanders
handily won the New Hampshire primary over

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:47.100
Hillary Clinton, but ultimately was not the
Democratic nominee.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.699
So let's look at New Hampshire in New Hampshire,
there is a new poll that is getting a lot

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:54.989
of attention in which Bernie Sanders has passed.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:57.909
Joe Biden, the poll is from Gravis.

00:00:57.909 --> 00:01:03.049
Biden has been leading every New Hampshire
poll dating all the way back to who even knows,

00:01:03.049 --> 00:01:04.799
I mean a long time dating too.

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:08.079
Before he even announced that he was actually
running.

00:01:08.079 --> 00:01:15.609
And now we have one new poll in which Bernie
Sanders has passed Biden, sort of Bernie Sanders

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:26.369
at 21 Joe Biden at 15 but the poll has a 6.2%
margin of error and only 250 primary voters

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:31.210
were actually asked their view on who they
will vote for in the primary.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:37.100
So in reality, again, I'm just the Messenger
here, I'm just looking at what's on the screen.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:43.140
In reality, even though Bernie Sanders is
leading Joe Biden by six, the pole has a 6.2%

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.350
margin of error and it is a statistical tie.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:50.319
So some people are maybe making more of a
big deal out of this poll than they should.

00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:57.280
This is one poll within the margin of error,
but the trend has been a clearly anti Biden

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.520
trend in New Hampshire.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.450
Now, some other important caveats, I'm not
being positive or negative.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.639
I'm not a Biden guy.

00:02:03.639 --> 00:02:05.850
I'm a Bernie Warren Guy.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.210
I'm just being a realist.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.620
New Hampshire is Vermont's neighbor.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.680
Bernie is from New Hampshire.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.830
Winning New Hampshire in 2020 would not really
be a sign of anything for Bernie Sanders in

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:23.050
the sense that he crushed Hillary there in
the 2016 primary 60 to 38 and ultimately lost

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.050
the nomination.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.220
I would actually go further.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:31.020
If Bernie Sanders doesn't win New Hampshire
on February 11th of 2020, it's probably a

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:36.440
really bad sign for him in the 2020 Democratic
primary because it is a state, uh, that he

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.480
so easily won in 2016.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.860
So this is valuable in showing us a trend
line.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.590
It's valuable in that every primary is worth
some delegates and the more you win, the more

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.370
delegates you have towards the nomination.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:53.989
We just don't want to give more value to this
one poll in one state that Bernie won in 2016

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:55.670
anyway than it deserves.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.800
Now there's another interesting data point
from this poll when it comes to electability,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.599
uh, and the matchups versus Trump.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:11.040
New Hampshire voters say they'd vote for Bernie
over Trump 51 to 41, and for Biden over Trump

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.030
by an even bigger margin, 53 to 40.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.819
This is interesting because if you look back
at 2016 in the general, Hillary Clinton won

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:23.730
New Hampshire, but she won by 0.37%.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.910
If the Democratic candidate wins New Hampshire
in 2020 it doesn't matter who it is at this

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:34.250
point by three by four, by six, by 10 by 13
the way this poll is suggesting this would

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:39.810
be a massive signal in terms of the broader
election, but that being said, it's early.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:47.239
Both of these margins, 51 41 and 53 40 they
include like seven to 8% undecided or third

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:48.780
party voters.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.739
That is not going to be the reality by election
day, but even if all of the currently undecided

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:59.849
and third party voters shift to Trump by election
day, these would still be bigger wins in 2020

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:05.340
in New Hampshire Than Hillary Clinton had,
and it could be a pretty big signal for 2020

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.890
it's all speculative.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:08.569
It's all hypothetical.

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:09.700
It's very early.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:10.700
Everyone must vote.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.659
No matter what you think is likely to happen
in your state.

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:20.580
Assume everybody should operate under the
belief which may end up not being wrong, that

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.100
every state could be 50 50 and your vote could
make the difference.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.900
I mean literally speaking, that's not going
to be the case, but that's the attitude every

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.100
single one of us needs to have.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.270
I'll be voting in the primary.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.950
I'll be voting in the general, even though
I vote here in Massachusetts.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:38.080
Once again, as far as New Hampshire is concerned,
this is one poll, relatively small sample

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.210
size, statistical tie.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.960
Nothing can be assumed.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.010
Every vote has to be cast and counted.

